  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nThe current approach uses local search and random perturbations, which can get stuck in local optima. Our strategy combines multi-resolution optimization with an affine parameterization to capture the global structure of the optimal solution. We hypothesize that the optimal function h(x) has a periodic component and an aperiodic component, and affine parameterization allows us to effectively explore both globally and locally.\n\nKey innovations:\n- Project the function onto a subspace of affine transformations to capture structural similarities\n- Use stochastic rounding to ensure feasibility at every step\n- Solve a transformed optimization problem that enforces constraints analytically\n- Switch between global and local search based on algorithmic signals\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\nWe represent h(x) as an affine transformation of a triangular kernel. The optimization problem is transformed into minimizing:\nJ(\u03b8) = max_k ||T_\u03b8(x) * (I - T\u03c6(x+k))||_\u221e\nsubject to:\n\u222bh(x)dx = 1, 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\nWhere T\u03b8 is an affine transformation, I is identity, and \u03c6 is the triangular kernel.\n\nThe transformed problem is strictly convex in the parameter space, allowing for reliable gradient-based optimization.\n\n3. Implementation:\na. Precomputation: Create lookup tables for fixed grid computations\nb. Phase 1: Initialize with parent solution using triangular kernel transformation\nc. Phase 2: Global search with polynomial schedule for random flips\nd. Phase 3: Local refinement with trust-region steps\ne. Phase 4: Progressive upsampling with integral projection\n\n4. Search Choices:\nUse adaptive: random scale factor starts at 0.1 and decreases until 0.001\nGlobal search: Block size 3 with replacement probability 0.7\nLocal search: Minimal step size 0.001 with reduction when improvement stalls\n\n5. Budget Allocation:\nTotal time: 1000 seconds\n- Global optimization: 400 seconds\n- Local refinement: 400 seconds\n- Final verification: 200 seconds\n\n6. Validation:\n - Ensure all h[i] in [0,1] by analytical integration\n - Re-evaluate candidate using FFT correlation\n - Maintain parent solution as fallback if loss increases by >0.0001",
